    "title": "Lung architecture",
    "creator": "Isabel Uwagboe",
    "affiliation": "King’s College London",
    "caption": "The intricate cellular architecture of the deep lung, revealed through immunofluorescence microscopy.",
    "alt": "Branching orange and gold structures surround dark air spaces in human lung tissue, with blue cell nuclei and scattered bright green cells.",
    "recognition": "Winner · KCL Nikon Imaging Centre competition, 2024",
    "description": "The image shows a snapshot of the cellular dynamics of the epithelium occurring with the human lung, using human precision cut lung slice (hPCLS) samples. The control tissue sample was cultured for 5 days before fixing, used to observe and analyse the differences between normal vs disease human lung. The final image is the result of optimising numerous immunofluorescence protocols, largely accounting for the thicker (400um) sample size.\n\nThe image was captured at the Nikon Imaging Centre (NIC, King’s College London), on the Nikon W1 Spinning Disk Confocal microscope (SoRA super-resolution disk). \n\nThis image was the winner of the KCL NIC competition 2024.",
