  {
    "accession": "S-BIAD3554",
    "slug": "geometry-of-mitosis",
    "title": "The geometry of mitosis",
    "creator": "Bhagya Lakshmi Ravindranath",
    "affiliation": "Danish Cancer Institute",
    "caption": "A floral arrangement of dividing cells reveals the geometry of mitosis.",
    "alt": "A red and green microscopic flower sits between two silvery butterfly-like chromosome arrangements on black.",
    "recognition": "Joint winner · 3rd Danish BioImaging Microscopy Image Contest, 2026",
    "imageUuid": "2f44c91f-e9ff-425f-8325-249d930ee79b",
    "representationUuid": "42e39c80-3e41-4cdf-876f-907247b6710e",
    "description": "This image shows the intricate geometry of mitosis. The primary image in the middle shows a floral pattern constructed from a metaphase cell. The \"petals\" are composed of kinetochore region dual-stained for proteins Astrin (green) and CENP-C (red), arranged into a symmetrical flower motif. The images at the corners show mitotic cell showing unaligned chromosomes (grey) with kinetochores (green/red) forming a whimsical butterfly-like shape. These images are obtained from the Zeiss LSM800 confocal microscope in the DCI core facility.\n\nThis image was a joint winner of the 3rd Danish BioImaging Microscopy Image Contest 2026",
