  {
    "accession": "S-BIAD3971",
    "slug": "winters-anatomy",
    "title": "Winter’s Anatomy — When Lungs Become Trees",
    "creator": "Claudia Victoria Benke",
    "affiliation": "Phase Contrast Imaging Flagship Node Trieste",
    "caption": "The branching airways of a human lung echo a leafless winter tree.",
    "alt": "A finely branching grey lung airway structure casts a tree-like shadow across a pale background.",
    "recognition": "Winner · Euro-BioImaging Four Seasons of the Invisible — Winter",
    "imageUuid": "d0d3e20d-58ff-4ebf-aa1f-bc239fbce19b",
    "representationUuid": "26df8e4f-32ce-4d3b-8502-1eeeffcb5bc0",
    "description": "This image reveals the branching airway system of a human lung, resembling a leafless tree in winter. Captured after death, the once dynamic network of bronchi and bronchioles appears suspended in stillness its structure intact, but its function gone.\nLike winter trees stripped of foliage, the lung’s hidden architecture is fully exposed. Synchrotron phase-contrast CT uncovers fine peripheral airways that remain invisible to conventional imaging. In this frozen state, the lung becomes a quiet reflection of winter itself: a moment where life pauses, revealing the elegance of its underlying design.\n\nWinning Image of Euro-BioImaging's Contest: Four Seasons of the Invisible - Winter.",
    "licence": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
    "licenceLabel": "Creative Commons Attribution 4.0",
    "zarrUri": "https://livingobjects.ebi.ac.uk/bioimaging-integrator-data/S-BIAD3971/d0d3e20d-58ff-4ebf-aa1f-bc239fbce19b/26df8e4f-32ce-4d3b-8502-1eeeffcb5bc0.ome.zarr/0",
